// @emit-error field access but no struct type declared
// XFAIL, REJECTS TOO MUCH. A NUMERIC literal-union array is fine as a `const`, and refused in a
// function SIGNATURE. The string-literal-union spelling of the same program compiles and runs.
//
//   type K = 1.5 | 2.5
//   const xs: K[] = [1.5]          // fine — `xs.length`, `xs[0]`, all of it
//   function mk(): K[] { … }       // emitProgram: field access but no struct type declared
//   function n(xs: K[]) { … }      // same
//   function first(xs: K[]): K { } // emitProgram: index access but array type not collected
//
// THE CONST HALF ONLY STARTED WORKING RECENTLY, which is what makes the asymmetry worth pinning
// rather than shrugging at. `collectA`'s `f64`/`i64` rungs took `nodeTyArrayElemRepName(i)`,
// which answers `""` for a `TyArray` whose element is a `TyUnion` of numeric `TyLit`s —
// `tyIsLitUnion` demands `litKind == "str"`, so the numeric ones match nothing. The node fell
// through to `checkArrName` and was rejected. Those rungs now take the NAME as a second opinion
// (`arrays/elem-rep-name-silent-on-covered-nodes.vl`), which fixed every DECLARATION position.
//
// Function signatures are collected by a different route, and it was never fixed — so this is
// the same root cause surfacing at the one position the second opinion does not reach. Fixing it
// means teaching the signature path the numeric-litunion element rep, not adding another name
// fallback.
//
// The STRING control below is the whole argument that this is a completeness gap and not a
// deliberate decline: `type S = "a" | "b"` in the identical signature compiles, runs, and prints.
// Two literal-union element kinds, one of them arbitrarily unsupported at one position.
//
// WHEN IT CLOSES: drop the prefix, flip to `@run`, and assert `1` from `mk().length` alongside
// the control's `1`.
type K = 1.5 | 2.5

function mk(): K[] {
  return [1.5]
}

print(mk().length)
